        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Redwood Sealing Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Redwood is naturally rot-resistant, which leads people to under-protect it. What it is not resistant to is UV, and that is what turns it grey.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Rot Resistance Means No Sealer Needed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Redwood's natural extractives resist decay and insects; they do nothing about ultraviolet light. Left unsealed, redwood greys within a season or two and the surface fibres break down. A UV-blocking sealer is what preserves the colour that made the wood worth buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Clear Sealer for Colour Protection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fully clear finishes contain no pigment, and pigment is what actually blocks UV. A clear sealer repels water and lets the wood grey underneath it anyway. If keeping the warm redwood tone matters, a transparent or semi-transparent tinted product is the minimum.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Tannin Bleed on New Boards</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Redwood extractives leach out and stain adjacent concrete, painted trim, and lighter surfaces below. Clean and brighten new redwood before sealing to draw the surface tannins out, and expect some bleed in the first rain regardless.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Over-Applying to Dense Heartwood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tight-grained redwood heartwood absorbs far less than the label assumes. Flooded, the excess stays on the surface, dries sticky, and collects dirt. Apply thin, work a few boards at a time, and wipe back anything that has not soaked in within fifteen minutes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">How do you keep redwood looking new?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">To keep redwood looking new, apply a UV-protective sealer or semi-transparent stain within 2-4 weeks of installation, and reapply every 1-2 years on decks or every 2-3 years on fences. Between applications, clean the wood annually with a dedicated deck cleaner or a solution of oxygen bleach and water — avoid chlorine bleach, which can damage redwood fibers. For furniture, wipe down monthly and reapply a penetrating oil finish like Cabot Australian Timber Oil annually. The key is consistent maintenance rather than neglecting the wood and trying to restore it later.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

